## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to generalize DP with an extra resource dimension and reconstruction, a common pattern in grid path problems with constraints. It also probes edge-case handling when i or j is at 0 and when t=0. 

## Key Concepts
- DP with an extra dimension for resource usage (K diagonals)
- 3-way transitions and path reconstruction
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPath(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const INF = 1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array.from({ length: m }, () => Array(K+1).fill(INF)));
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array.from({ length: m }, () => Array(K+1).fill(null)));
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0 && t === 0) continue;
        let best = INF, pick = null;
        if (i > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t] < best) { best = dp[i-1][j][t]; pick = [i-1, j, t]; }
        if (j > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t] < best) { best = dp[i][j-1][t]; pick = [i, j-1, t]; }
        if (i > 0 && j > 0 && t > 0 && dp[i-1][j-1][t-1] < best) { best = dp[i-1][j-1][t-1]; pick = [i-1, j-1, t-1]; }
        if (best < INF) {
          dp[i][j][t] = best + grid[i][j];
          if (pick) parent[i][j][t] = pick;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // find best end state
  let bestCost = INF, endT = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
    if (dp[n-1][m-1][t] < bestCost) { bestCost = dp[n-1][m-1][t]; endT = t; }
  }
  // reconstruct path
  const path = [];
  let i = n-1, j = m-1, t = endT;
  while (i !== 0 || j !== 0 || t !== 0) {
    path.push([i, j]);
    const p = parent[i][j][t];
    if (!p) break;
    [i, j, t] = p;
  }
  path.push([0,0]);
  path.reverse();
  return { cost: bestCost, path };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to reduce space to O(m K) using rolling arrays?
- How would you handle negative costs or unreachable endpoints?

## Why This Is Asked

Explores a novel variation of LPS with deletions, forcing a 3D DP and careful reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- 3D DP: i, j bounds plus remaining deletions t.
- Transitions handle match, deletion of either end, and base cases.
- Reconstruction by tracing choices from dp[0][n-1][k].
- Space optimization via rolling t or two 2D layers.

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxLPSWithDeletions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][i][0] = 1;
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len - 1 < n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (s[i] === s[j]) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], (i+1<=j-1? dp[i+1][j-1][t] : 0) + 2);
        }
        if (t > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i+1][j][t-1], dp[i][j-1][t-1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0][n-1][k] || 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize to O(nk) space while preserving O(n^2 k) time?
- How does the solution change if deletions must be exactly k?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests a foundational dynamic programming pattern—selecting k non-adjacent elements with maximum sum. It assesses understanding of state definition, transition logic, and reconstruction, making it a classic beginner DP problem.

## Key Concepts
- **State definition**: dp[i][j] represents the optimal sum for the first i elements with exactly j selections
- **Transition logic**: Compare skipping the current element vs. taking it (with the appropriate previous state)
- **Backtracking**: Reconstruct chosen indices by comparing states during backtracking
- **Feasibility constraint**: Maximum selections possible in i elements is floor((i+1)/2)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumNonAdjacent(A, m) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(m + 1).fill(NEG));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j <= Math.min(m, Math.floor((i + 1) / 2)); j++) {
      const take = A[i - 1] + (i >= 2 ? dp[i - 2][j - 1] : (j - 1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG));
      const skip = dp[i - 1][j];
      dp[i][j] = Math.max(skip, take);
    }
  }

  const result = dp[n][m];
  const indices = [];
  let i = n, j = m;

  while (i > 0 && j > 0) {
    const take = A[i - 1] + (i >= 2 ? dp[i - 2][j - 1] : (j - 1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG));
    if (dp[i][j] === take) {
      indices.push(i - 1);
      j--;
      i -= 2;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }

  return { sum: result, indices: indices.reverse() };
}
```

**Time Complexity:** O(n × m)
**Space Complexity:** O(n × m), or O(m) with optimization.

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests partitioning a sequence into exactly k blocks where block value is non-linear in length. It requires a careful DP with reconstruction and shows understanding of prefix sums and state transitions.

## Key Concepts

- DP over partitions
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- Non-linear block value and reconstruction
- Time/space trade-offs: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarraysProduct(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + a[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const pick = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const val = dp[j-1][t-1] + (i - j + 1) * (pref[i] - pref[j-1]);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          pick[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize to O(nk) time with monotone queue or divide-and-conquer tricks?
- How would you handle allowing at most k blocks and a penalty for unused blocks?

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This problem evaluates understanding of weighted interval scheduling with a cardinality constraint—a fundamental DP pattern in scheduling and resource allocation. It also tests the ability to reconstruct the actual solution, not just compute the optimal value.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with an exact cardinality constraint
- Precomputing p(i) via binary search after sorting intervals by their right endpoint
- Two-dimensional DP table indexed by interval position and number selected
- Backtracking through the DP table to recover the chosen intervals

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueWithKIntervals(intervals, A, k) {
    intervals.sort((a, b) => a.r - b.r);
    const n = intervals.length;
    const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
    const selected = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(false));

    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        const [l, r] = [intervals[i-1].l, intervals[i-1].r];
        const v = intervals[i-1].value;
        const p = findPrev(intervals, i-1);

        for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
            if (dp[p][t-1] + v > dp[i-1][t]) {
                dp[i][t] = dp[p][t-1] + v;
                selected[i][t] = true;
            } else {
                dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
            }
        }
    }

    const result = [];
    let i = n, t = k;
    while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
        if (selected[i][t]) {
            result.unshift(i - 1);
            t--;
            i = p;
        } else {
            i--;
        }
    }

    return { maxValue: dp[n][k], indices: result };
}

function findPrev(intervals, idx) {
    let lo = 0, hi = idx;
    while (lo < hi) {
        const mid = Math.ceil((lo + hi) / 2);
        if (intervals[mid].r <= intervals[idx].l) lo = mid;
        else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return lo;
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- Preprocessing p(i): O(n log n) via sorting and binary search
- DP computation: O(n × k)
- Reconstruction: O(n + k)
- Space: O(n × k) for the DP table

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to handle non-overlapping subarrays with non-linear segment values (min over range). Combines DP over partitions with a range-min query structure. Encourages thinking about precomputation and reconstruction trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- DP over partitions with fixed k
- Range minimum query for B
- Prefix sums for A
- Path reconstruction and complexity considerations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: compute prefA, RMQ for B, then fill dp[i][t]
for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
  for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
    let best = -Infinity
    for (let j=1; j<=i; j++) {
      best = Math.max(best, dp[j-1][t-1] + (prefA[i]-prefA[j-1]) - minB(j,i))
    }
    dp[i][t] = best
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize to O(nk log n) using a segment tree? 
- How does tie-breaking affect reconstruction when multiple segment sets yield same value?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests modeling of selecting multiple disjoint subarrays with a fixed cost and a cap on count, including interval reconstruction. It blends standard DP on subarrays with a knapsack-like limit and requires careful state design.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix index i and subarray count t
- Prefix sums for fast subarray sums
- Transitions: skip vs end-subarray
- Reconstruction via back-pointers; space/time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for (let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      par[i][t] = {type:'skip'};
      if (t>0){
        for (let L=1; L<=i; L++){
          const prev = dp[i-L][t-1];
          if (prev <= -Infinity/2) continue;
          const cand = prev + (pref[i]-pref[i-L]) - c;
          if (cand > dp[i][t]){
            dp[i][t] = cand;
            par[i][t] = {type:'end', end:i, len:L};
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would walk back from (n, argmax_t)
  return {maxValue: dp[n].reduce((a,b)=> Math.max(a,b), -Infinity)};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time and O(nk) space, and then reduce to O(nk) time and O(k) space?
- How would you extend for at most k subarrays with variable per-subarray costs or negative values?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This problem assesses your ability to handle non-trivial path constraints in dynamic programming, specifically exact turn counting. It also requires path reconstruction, reflecting real-world interview complexity where identifying a solution isn't sufficient—you must also provide the actual path.

## Key Concepts
- **4D DP state** capturing both position and path history: `(i, j, t, dir)` tracks where you are, how many turns you've made, and your incoming direction
- **Turn counting** via conditional transitions: staying in the same direction doesn't increment the turn count, while switching does
- **Backpointer-based reconstruction** stores the predecessor for each state, enabling full path recovery

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithKTurns(grid, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () =>
    Array.from({length: m}, () =>
      Array.from({length: k + 1}, () => [-Infinity, -Infinity])
    )
  );
  dp[0][0][0][0] = dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];

  const parent = Array.from({length: n}, () =>
    Array.from({length: m}, () =>
      Array.from({length: k + 1}, () => [null, null])
    )
  );

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;
        if (j > 0) {
          const fromRight = dp[i][j-1][t][0];
          const fromDown = t > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t-1][1] : -Infinity;
          if (fromRight >= fromDown) {
            dp[i][j][t][0] = fromRight + grid[i][j];
            parent[i][j][t][0] = [i, j-1, t, 0];
          } else {
            dp[i][j][t][0] = fromDown + grid[i][j];
            parent[i][j][t][0] = [i, j-1, t-1, 1];
          }
        }
        if (i > 0) {
          const fromDown = dp[i-1][j][t][1];
          const fromRight = t > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t-1][0] : -Infinity;
          if (fromDown >= fromRight) {
            dp[i][j][t][1] = fromDown + grid[i][j];
            parent[i][j][t][1] = [i-1, j, t, 1];
          } else {
            dp[i][j][t][1] = fromRight + grid[i][j];
            parent[i][j][t][1] = [i-1, j, t-1, 0];
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const endDir = dp[n-1][m-1][k][0] >= dp[n-1][m-1][k][1] ? 0 : 1;
  const maxSum = dp[n-1][m-1][k][endDir];
  const path = [];
  let cur = [n-1, m-1, k, endDir];

  while (cur) {
    path.unshift([cur[0], cur[1]]);
    cur = parent[cur[0]][cur[1]][cur[2]][cur[3]];
  }

  return { sum: maxSum, path };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP state augmentation for a path with a constrained number of turns, combining path optimization with reconstruction. It demands careful handling of transitions when direction changes. 

## Key Concepts
- DP on grid paths with turn constraints
- 4D state dp[i][j][t][dir]
- Reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time: O(n*m*k); Space: O(n*m*k)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithKTurns(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () =>
    Array.from({length: m}, () => Array.from({length: K+1}, () => [NEG, NEG]))
  );
  dp[0][0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        const val = grid[i][j];
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t][0], dp[i][j-1][t][0] + val);
          if (t > 0) dp[i][j][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t][0], dp[i][j-1][t-1][1] + val);
        }
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t][1], dp[i-1][j][t][1] + val);
          if (t > 0) dp[i][j][t][1] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t][1], dp[i-1][j][t-1][0] + val);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would backtrack using stored choices (omitted for brevity)
  return {dp, best: Math.max(...Array.from({length:K+1}, (_,t)=>Math.max(dp[n-1][m-1][t][0], dp[n-1][m-1][t][1])))};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This problem evaluates dynamic programming expertise across string partitioning with fixed segment counts, numeric parsing constraints, and solution reconstruction—fundamental skills for real-world interview scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic programming over string prefixes and partition counts
- Handling substring numeric values with leading-zero validation
- Solution path reconstruction for determining optimal cut positions
- Time and space complexity optimization techniques

## Code Example

```javascript
function minSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;

  // Helper to get numeric value of substring [l, r)
  const getValue = (l, r) => {
    if (s[l] === '0' && r - l > 1) return null; // leading zero
    return Number(s.slice(l, r));
  };

  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const parent = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let j = t - 1; j < i; j++) {
        const val = getValue(j, i);
        if (val !== null && dp[j][t-1] + val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[j][t-1] + val;
          parent[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct cuts
  const cuts = [];
  let curr = n, currT = k;
  while (currT > 0) {
    const prev = parent[curr][currT];
    cuts.push(prev);
    curr = prev;
    currT--;
  }
  cuts.pop(); // remove 0
  cuts.reverse();

  return { minSum: dp[n][k], cuts };
}
```

## Notes

- The DP explores all possible cut positions while respecting the k-substring constraint
- Leading zero validation is crucial for correctness
- The parent array enables O(k) reconstruction of optimal cuts

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This problem tests your ability to design multi-dimensional DP by combining deletion constraints with pairwise scoring across adjacent characters. It evaluates state space design, transition logic, and reconstruction from a full solution path.

## Key Concepts
- **State design**: A 3D DP tracks position i (0 to n), deletions used d (0 to k), and the last kept character L (0–25, or 26 for none).
- **Transition logic**: The delete transition preserves state; the keep transition accumulates score only when a previous character exists, using W[prev][cur].
- **Reconstruction**: Backtracking from the optimal endpoint to determine which characters were deleted.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScore(s, W, k) {
    const n = s.length, NONE = 26;
    const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () =>
        Array.from({length: k+1}, () =>
            Array(NONE+1).fill(-Infinity)));
    dp[0][0][NONE] = 0;

    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
            for (let L = 0; L <= NONE; L++) {
                if (dp[i][d][L] === -Infinity) continue;
                // Delete
                if (d < k) dp[i+1][d+1][L] = Math.max(dp[i+1][d+1][L], dp[i][d][L]);
                // Keep
                const cur = s.charCodeAt(i) - 97;
                const gain = L === NONE ? 0 : W[L][cur];
                dp[i+1][d][cur] = Math.max(dp[i+1][d][cur], dp[i][d][L] + gain);
            }
        }
    }
    // Find best among d ≤ k
    let best = 0, bestD = 0, bestL = NONE;
    for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++)
        for (let L = 0; L <= NONE; L++)
            if (dp[n][d][L] > best) { best = dp[n][d][L]; bestD = d; bestL = L; }
    return { score: best, deletions: bestD, lastKept: bestL };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- **Memory optimization**: Can you reduce space to O(27·k) using rolling arrays?
- **Negative weights**: How would you handle cases where W contains negative values—would your approach change?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over time with multiple constraints: multiple transactions, reconstruction, and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Two DP states: cash (not holding) and hold (holding).
- Transitions model buy/sell with transaction count t.
- Base cases initialize day 0; backtracking recovers trade days.
- Space optimization possible to O(k) with rolling arrays.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfit(prices, k){
  const n = prices.length;
  if (n===0 || k===0) return 0;
  const cash = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  const hold = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1e15));
  for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) cash[0][t] = (t===0)?0:-1e15;
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for (let t=0; t<=k; t++){
      cash[i][t] = cash[i-1][t];
      if (t>0) cash[i][t] = Math.max(cash[i][t], hold[i-1][t] + prices[i-1]);
      hold[i][t] = hold[i-1][t];
      if (t>0) hold[i][t] = Math.max(hold[i][t], cash[i-1][t-1] - prices[i-1]);
    }
  }
  let best = 0;
  for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) best = Math.max(best, cash[n][t]);
  return best;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you recover the exact trade days from the DP table?
- How does the solution change if k >= n/2 (unlimited transactions)?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests DP partitioning with a non-trivial per-segment cost that depends on both sum and min, plus reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP with exact k segments
- Precompute minVal(l,r) in O(n^2)
- Transition uses prefix sums and minVal; reconstruct with parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxCost(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  // minVal[l][r]
  const minVal = Array.from({length:n+2}, ()=>Array(n+2).fill(0));
  for(let l=1;l<=n;l++){
    let m = A[l-1];
    minVal[l][l] = m;
    for(let r=l;r<=n;r++){
      m = (r===l) ? A[l-1] : Math.min(m, A[r-1]);
      minVal[l][r] = m;
    }
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){ dp[i][1] = (pref[i]-pref[0]) * minVal[1][i]; par[i][1] = 0; }
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + (pref[i]-pref[p]) * minVal[p+1][i];
        if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n][k]};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests DP on grids with a minimax objective, reconstruction, and practical edge handling (blocked cells).

## Key Concepts
- DP on 2D grid with a minimax recurrence
- Path reconstruction via predecessor tracking
- Space optimization opportunities (1D row/column DP)
- INF handling for blockers

## Code Example
```javascript
function minMaxRisk(r) {
  const n = r.length, m = r[0].length;
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(m).fill(INF));
  const from = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(m).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = r[0][0];
  for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) { dp[0][j] = Math.max(dp[0][j-1], r[0][j]); from[0][j] = 0; }
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) { dp[i][0] = Math.max(dp[i-1][0], r[i][0]); from[i][0] = 1; }
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) {
      const up = Math.max(dp[i-1][j], r[i][j]);
      const left = Math.max(dp[i][j-1], r[i][j]);
      if (up <= left) { dp[i][j] = up; from[i][j] = 0; } else { dp[i][j] = left; from[i][j] = 1; }
    }
  }
  return {minMaxRisk: dp[n-1][m-1], parent: from};
}
```

